\begin_layout Standard
In this chapter a review of the field of van der Waals' materials is presented.
 Graphene's properties and prospects are discussed and the motivation for
 van der Waals' heterostructures outlined.
 Characterisation of graphene and heterostructures by different methods
 will be reviewed, with particular focus on techniques around transmission
 electron microscopy (TEM).
 FIB sample preparation for TEM imaging reviewed, with particular attention
 paid to the challenges of this method when applied to beam sensitive 2D
 materials and its limitations.
 Common image processing and analysis techniques as applied to cross section
 images of heterostructures are discussed in the penultimate section.
 The final section gives an overview of density functional theory (DFT)
 calculations for van der Waals' heterostructures.
 DFT was carried out by coauthors of papers in the following chapters but
 was not part of the project.
 Nevertheless, it is the only technique which provides useful comparison
 to the quantitative STEM data presented in subsequent chapters, and so
 an understanding of it's merits and shortcomings is useful.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Van der Waals' materials and heterostructures
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is perhaps a hallmark of success that the name 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

graphene
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 means different things to different communities: from a strictly free-standing
 single atomic plane of carbon, to a vast field of research encompassing
 all atomically thin crystals utilising van der Waals' bonding.
 For the purposes of this work, graphene is a two dimensional allotrope
 of carbon: a single layer of carbon atoms held together by sp
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

 hybridised bonds to form a hexagonal lattice.[ref] These sheets can be sequentia
lly stacked together to form bilayer[ref], trilayer[ref] e.t.c graphene up
 to bulk graphite[ref], wherein each graphene sheet is known as a Basal
 plane.[refs] Van der Waals' materials are defined as thick crystals composed
 of many layers of graphene or other 2D crystals, such as hexagonal boron
 nitride (hBN) and transition metal dichalcogenides (TMDCs).
 We will discuss 2D crystals later in this section.
 It should be noted that bulk graphite, and all bulk crystals with homogenous
 composition and van der Waals' bonding between atomicly thin sheets, are
 included in the definition of van der Waals' materials.
 An interesting extension of this definition occurs when different 2D crystals
 are stacked upon one another to form van der Waals' heterostructures.
 We address these the final part of this section.
 The papers presented in the following chapters characterise both van der
 Waals' materials and van der Waals' heterostructures, and not isolated
 sheets of 2D materials.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
The properties and potential applications of graphene
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In 1947 P R Wallace attempted to explain the electronic properties of graphite
 by considering the electronic structure of the individual graphene sheets,
 finding each 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

to be a semi-conductor with zero activation energy
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wallace1947theband"

\end_inset

 The properties of graphene have proved to be far stranger.
 In 2004 Nobel laureates Prof Andre Geim and Prof Konstantin Novoselov were
 the first to successfully isolate and characterise free standing graphene
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "novoselov2004electric"

\end_inset

, although it should be acknowledged that monolayer graphite was known prior
 to this
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "geim2007therise"

\end_inset

.
 They did this using the 'Scotch tape' method, where micromechanical cleavage
 is achieved using sticky tape to pull apart thick highly oriented pyrolytic
 graphite until it is atomically thin.
 The first electronic transport measurements not only showed that graphene's
 band structure has 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Dirac cones
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 at the K-points of the Brillouin zone, and that charge carriers close to
 the centre of the cone behave as massless Dirac fermions, but that the
 anomalous quantum Hall effect and Berry's phase were experimentally measureable
 in high quality graphene flakes.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "novoselov2005twodimensional,zhang2005experimental"

\end_inset

 Since then graphene has been a hot topic of research with the original
 Science paper recieving over 21,000 citations, placing it in the top 100
 most cited papers.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "vannoorden2014thetop"

\end_inset

 Later still, single layer graphene exhibited carrier mobilities of up to
 200,000 cm
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

V
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
-1
\end_layout

\end_inset

 s
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
-1
\end_layout

\end_inset

at low temperature in the highest quality devices 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "chen2008intrinsic,bolotin2008ultrahigh"

\end_inset

, but (perhaps more pertinently) mobilities upwards of 15,000 cm
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

V
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
-1
\end_layout

\end_inset

 s
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
-1
\end_layout

\end_inset

at room temperature
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "geim2007therise"

\end_inset

.
 It was not long before it was realised that the thin silicon dioxide substrate,
 which was relied up to reveal single layer graphene under the optical microscop
e
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "blake2007makinggraphene"

\end_inset

 and insulate graphene from the silicon back gate
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "novoselov2005twodimensional,zhang2005experimental"

\end_inset

, was breaking up the 2D electron gas in graphene through surface roughness,
 small areas of inhomogenous charging in the silicon dioxide surface and
 impurities.
 The device performance was significantly improved by inserting a thin layer
 of insulating hexagonal boron nitride (hBN) between the graphene and the
 substrate.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "dean2010boronnitride"

\end_inset

This was the first instance of a van der Waals' heterostructure and heralded
 in a step change in the rapidly expanding field.
 
